## Central Limit Theorem - Dice Example

In a particular dice game, $N=500$ players roll a die once. The
distribution of scores is shown in the histogram below. This is an
example of a discrete uniform distribution. The red dots indicate the
probability $1/6$ associated with each score.

```{r}
score=sample(1:6,500,replace=TRUE) # randomly sample 500 numbers from the vector 1,2,3,4,5,6
hist(score,breaks=seq(0.5,6.5),freq=FALSE) # plot a histogram
points(c(1:6),rep(1/6,6),col=2) # add dots with x-values 1 to 6 and y-values 1/6
```

Now suppose that each player's score is the sum of two dice. The
distribution of scores is shown in the histogram below. This is an
example of a triangular distribution. That is, $X$ the total from two
rolls of a dice satisfies\
$$ P(X=x) = \frac{\min\{x-1, 13-x \}}{36} \qquad (x=2,3,\ldots, 12). $$\
The red dots indicate the probability of each score (assuming the dice
rolls are independent).

```{r}
score=sample(1:6,500,replace=TRUE)+sample(1:6,500,replace=TRUE) # the players scores sampled at random
hist(score,breaks=seq(1.5,12.5),ylim=c(0,0.2),freq=FALSE) # plot a histogram
points(c(2:12),c(1:6,5:1)/36,col=2) # add dots to show the theoretical proabilities
```

Now suppose each player's score is the sum of five dice. The
distribution of scores is shown in the histogram below.

```{r}
score=sample(1:6,500,replace=TRUE)
for (i in 2:5){
  score=score+sample(1:6,500,replace=TRUE)
}
hist(score,seq(4.5,30.5),freq=FALSE)
```

